Exqlite 0.40.0: never block a scheduler in the statement destructor.

Applied to deps/exqlite by `mix deps.patch` (@dep_patches in mix.exs), which
runs after every `mix deps.get`, so dev, CI and the Docker image all build it.
The Dockerfile checks the result with patches/exqlite/destructor_probe.exs, and
test/mydia/repo/exqlite_patch_test.exs fails any test run on the unpatched
NIF. The Nix package still builds upstream Exqlite.

Dev and CI ran upstream until 2026-09-21. There the stall was the cause of
SQLite `database is locked` flakes (.github/ci-flakes.md,
CandidatePromotionTest), because a lock holder that drops a statement of a
waiting connection waits as long as the waiter does.

Retiring it. A bump stops `mix deps.get` at the version pin, leaving the new,
unpatched release in deps/exqlite. Run
`mix test test/mydia/repo/exqlite_patch_test.exs` against it. If that passes,
upstream fixed the destructor: delete this directory, its @dep_patches entry
and its Dockerfile step. If it fails, rebase this patch and move the pin.

The bug. statement_type_destructor locks the connection mutex before
finalizing. Destructors run on whichever scheduler drops the last reference,
usually a normal one during GC. A query busy-waiting on another connection's
write lock holds that mutex for the whole busy timeout (30s in Mydia). If the
write lock's owner drops a stale statement from the waiting connection, it
cannot commit until the waiter gives up: every write stalls for exactly
busy_timeout, and the stuck scheduler blocks thread progress for the whole VM.
On a production install this froze Mydia for 30-60s about twenty times a day,
stalling video segments and every GraphQL call.

The fix. The destructor only trylocks. When the connection is busy it queues
the sqlite3_stmt and returns. The next connection_acquire_lock on that
connection finalizes the queue, and the connection destructor does too, so a
connection nobody calls again still cleans up. mix.exs forces a source build;
otherwise elixir_make downloads the precompiled upstream NIF and this patch
never takes effect.

